  |title A steward named his own prescription as prescription, in the sentence he was making it

  |summary
    Mid-instruction on how to delegate, he flagged that the instruction was itself
    the failure he was warning against, and left both standing.

  |account
    He had just supplied a template for delegating a small task to a Sonnet agent —
    the wording, the framing, what to say. Then, without being challenged:

    *"I know *I'm* being prescriptive by telling you how to delegate, and *I* am the
    one that failed earlier by giving sonnet too much allowance and having it blow
    back on me, but I guess I'm trying to point out that we're going to need to
    continue to delegate the right way or it will spiral into thoughtless coercion.
    Better to let agents pleasantly surprise us with figuring out what we would have
    done and more, and deal with the occasional reversible error introduction and
    propagation, in my estimation, even in light of the recent upsurge in error
    introduction...."*

    Three moves in one paragraph: the prescription is named as prescription; his own
    contributing error is named without being used as an excuse; and the trade is
    stated as a trade — occasional reversible error is the price, and it is worth
    paying — rather than as a rule.

    He did not withdraw the template. Naming its register was the whole correction,
    and it left the receiving agent free to use it or diverge.

  |what-would-have-happened-instead
    The instruction would have functioned. Marking it as prescription is what kept
    it from being inherited as method — the same repair the corpus asks agents to
    make, made by the steward on himself, unprompted, in real time.

  |provenance
    Reconstructed. Locatable verbatim in `.to-integrate/methodology-seeds-dialog-
